      "rank": 1,
      "ballot_id": "B007",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Peter W. Shor", "Andrew M. Steane", "Alexei Kitaev"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Quantum error correction showed how quantum information can be protected despite decoherence, turning a central obstacle into a precise physical framework. Its conceptual depth and influence across quantum physics are exceptional.",
        "attribution": "Shor and Steane established influential early codes, while Kitaev developed the topological approach to fault tolerance. These are distinct, central contributions, though the broader field has many important contributors.",
        "maturity": "The theory is well established and experiments have made substantial progress, but scalable fault-tolerant machines remain a work in progress. The foundational insight is already mature enough for recognition.",
        "uncertainties": ["The eventual balance between the foundational theory and later experimental demonstrations in an award citation is not yet clear."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B001",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Charles H. Bennett", "Gilles Brassard", "Artur Ekert"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Quantum key distribution made quantum measurement principles operational in a new way, with both fundamental significance and a clear experimental path. It established a durable field at the boundary of quantum physics and information.",
        "attribution": "Bennett and Brassard introduced the influential prepare-and-measure protocol; Ekert developed an entanglement-based approach connecting security to Bell tests. The three contributions are identifiable, though the field's later development involved many groups.",
        "maturity": "Protocols have been tested in laboratories and deployed over fiber and satellite links. Practical security still depends on implementation details, but the physical principles are well tested.",
        "uncertainties": ["The extent to which practical security claims can be separated from device imperfections remains an active issue."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B008",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Alexander A. Belavin", "Alexander B. Zamolodchikov", "Alexander M. Polyakov"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Two-dimensional conformal field theory gave exact methods for describing critical phenomena and became a powerful bridge between statistical mechanics and quantum field theory. Its reach and lasting conceptual influence are very large.",
        "attribution": "Belavin, Polyakov and Zamolodchikov's work established the influential framework and exact results. The proposed trio is strongly associated with this development, although conformal symmetry and critical phenomena have a broad history.",
        "maturity": "The framework has been extensively developed and applied across many systems over decades. Its foundations and significance are secure.",
        "uncertainties": ["A citation would need to distinguish this specific advance from the much wider history of renormalization and critical phenomena."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B044",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Yakir Aharonov", "Michael Berry"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The Aharonov–Bohm effect and geometric phase reveal that quantum phase has observable physical consequences beyond local forces. These are fundamental insights with wide influence across quantum physics.",
        "attribution": "Aharonov's work with Bohm identified the effect of electromagnetic potentials on quantum phase; Berry later established the general geometric phase in adiabatic evolution. The two contributions are related but distinct.",
        "maturity": "The effects have been observed in diverse settings and are now standard parts of quantum theory. Their interpretation and significance are mature.",
        "uncertainties": ["The bundled discovery joins related but historically separate results, so a single citation would require careful scope."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B035",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Hidetoshi Katori", "Jun Ye"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical lattice clocks brought atomic timekeeping to a new level of precision and opened a sensitive way to test fundamental physics. The work is experimentally demanding and has produced a robust platform.",
        "attribution": "Katori developed the optical lattice clock concept and its key control of atomic frequency shifts; Ye made major advances in precision and clock performance. Their complementary contributions are well established.",
        "maturity": "Independent laboratories have demonstrated increasingly accurate clocks, with applications to relativistic geodesy and tests of fundamental constants. The technology and scientific uses continue to advance.",
        "uncertainties": ["The prize case depends on weighing a transformative measurement platform against the more foundational discoveries on this list."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B020",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Juan Ignacio Cirac", "Peter Zoller"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Theoretical proposals for trapped-ion quantum computing and atomic quantum simulation showed how controlled atomic systems could implement quantum gates and emulate many-body physics. The work helped make quantum information an experimental science.",
        "attribution": "Cirac and Zoller jointly developed landmark proposals for trapped-ion gates and quantum simulation. Their close partnership makes attribution clear, though later experimental achievements belong to a much larger community.",
        "maturity": "Trapped-ion systems have demonstrated high-fidelity control and complex quantum operations, while scale remains challenging. The theoretical foundations have been confirmed and built upon.",
        "uncertainties": ["The ballot combines computing and simulation, whose experimental trajectories and scientific aims are not identical."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B012",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Alan H. Guth", "Andrei Linde", "Viatcheslav Mukhanov"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Inflation offers a compelling account of early-universe expansion and the origin of primordial structure, with consequences that can be compared to cosmological observations. If established, its explanatory reach is profound.",
        "attribution": "Guth introduced the inflationary scenario, Linde developed important versions, and Mukhanov derived key predictions for primordial fluctuations. Credit is spread across several independent theoretical contributions and subsequent developments.",
        "maturity": "Cosmic microwave background and large-scale-structure observations support several predictions associated with inflation, but no unique inflationary model has been confirmed.",
        "uncertainties": ["Direct evidence for inflation and a discriminating signature of a specific model remain absent."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B006",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Christopher Jarzynski", "Gavin E. Crooks", "Udo Seifert"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Fluctuation relations extended thermodynamics to small, driven systems where fluctuations matter, giving precise links between nonequilibrium work and equilibrium free energy. This is a substantial conceptual advance with close ties to experiment.",
        "attribution": "Jarzynski and Crooks formulated influential nonequilibrium work relations, while Seifert developed a broad stochastic-thermodynamic framework. Evans and collaborators also have foundational contributions, so a three-person citation would need a carefully defined scope.",
        "maturity": "The relations have been tested in several experimental settings and are established tools in nonequilibrium physics. The field continues to develop, including applications to quantum systems.",
        "uncertainties": ["The breadth of the field and its multiple independent formulations make a sharply bounded discovery and attribution difficult."]
      }
    }
  ]
